sec.p:
We employed the complex Morlet wavelet as the mother function for the continuous wavelet transform (CWT), following Torrence & Compo82. The Morlet wavelet is widely used in geophysical, climatological, and ecological applications82,93–96, as it provides an optimal balance between time and frequency localization93,95,97,98. Its Gaussian envelope ensures smooth spectral tapering, minimizing spectral leakage, while the sinusoidal carrier allows straightforward interpretation of both amplitude and phase. Furthermore, the Morlet wavelet has a simple and well-defined scale–frequency relationship (f ~ 1/s, where f is frequency and s is scale), which facilitates comparison with Fourier-based spectra and makes the identification of dominant periodicities more intuitive. This combination of properties makes it particularly suitable for detecting periodic/quasi-periodic oscillations and localized variability in natural records such as the Mediterranean forest pollen percentage time series21,96,99,100. The CWT of a regular, evenly spaced time series x(t) using a complex Morlet mother wavelet ψ is defined as82:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
