                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\psi (\eta )={\pi }^{(-1/4)}\exp (i\omega 0\eta )\exp (-{\eta }^{2/2})$$\end{document}ψ(η)=π(−1/4)exp(iω0η)exp(−η2/2)where ω0 = 2pi is the nondimensional central frequency. This formulation provides an optimal compromise between time and frequency localization and satisfies the admissibility condition (i.e., zero mean of the wavelet function)82,93,95,97,98. From the discretized form of the wavelet transform, we obtain a complex-valued matrix Wx(s,τ) representing the local time–frequency decomposition of the signal for a range of scales sj (j = 0,…,J) and times τi. The corresponding wavelet power spectrum (or wavelet variance) is defined as3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
